Conformance
Id	Clause
conformance.1	This specification defines one class of product, a reader, which reads the raw API bodies Claude Code writes of its Messages API calls [CLAUDE-CODE] and writes, for each call, one line of an OTLP JSON Lines file [OTEL-FILE-EXPORTER] holding one event [OTEL-GENAI]. The lines a reader writes are the input of the converter of the living-memory specification, spec/living-memory.mtsv, and MUST conform to it. The attributes of each call's request and response are written by the Messages API specification, messages.mtsv, in the provider's folder.
conformance.2	A reader conforms if, for every call in the raw API bodies, it writes the event by event.1, with each attribute of the Attributes sheet by the rules the sheet names for it and those the Messages API specification writes, and no other, and if it rejects all the raw API bodies it must reject.
conformance.3	The key words "MUST", "MUST NOT", "REQUIRED", "SHALL", "SHALL NOT", "SHOULD", "SHOULD NOT", "RECOMMENDED", "NOT RECOMMENDED", "MAY", and "OPTIONAL" in this specification are to be interpreted as described in BCP 14 [BCP14] when, and only when, they appear in all capitals, as shown here.
conformance.4	The Rules and the Attributes are normative. The first part of a rule's Id names what it governs: index the index file, event the event. A rule names each source it follows in brackets, as [CLAUDE-CODE]; the References list them, each marked normative or informative. This specification has no optional features: where a source leaves a choice to the implementation, a rule closes it.
conformance.5	Raw API bodies, index file, API request body and API response body are as defined in [CLAUDE-CODE]; event and attribute as in [OTEL-GENAI]; instrumentation library and natively instrumented as in [OTEL-GLOSSARY]; log record and LogsData as in [OTLP]; member, name and value as in [RFC8259]. In the Attributes sheet, Key is an attribute's key, and Rule names the rules that write it, or that state why it is absent.
Rules
Id	Rule
index.1	The raw API bodies are what Claude Code writes with OTEL_LOG_RAW_API_BODIES set to file:<dir>: in <dir>, the index file, index.jsonl, a JSON Lines file [JSONL] with one line for each successful response, holding timestamp, session_id, query_source, model, request_id, message_id, message_uuid, request_file and response_file; and the request and response files those name, the JSON-serialized Messages API request parameters and response [CLAUDE-CODE]. A reader MUST read each line of the index, in the index's order, with the request and response files it names. The page does not say whether the index names a file by an absolute path, as it does the event's body_ref [CLAUDE-CODE], so a reader MUST read a path that is not absolute in <dir>.
index.2	A reader MUST write the line's session_id, an identifier of the conversation that the raw API bodies hold, as gen_ai.conversation.id [OTEL-GENAI], and each other member of the line as anthropic.claude_code.{member}, its value converted by the living-memory specification's event.3, a property of Anthropic's product Claude Code [OTEL-SEMCONV].
index.3	Claude Code writes an index line only for a successful response, so a request attempt that failed has no event; and it redacts extended-thinking content from the bodies, so a reasoning part holds only what the body holds; and each request body holds the entire conversation history, so each event's input messages repeat those of the calls before it [CLAUDE-CODE].
index.4	An index line that is not a JSON object [JSONL] [RFC8259], or a request or response file that does not hold a JSON object, is non-conforming, as is a line whose request and response the Messages API specification rejects, or whose event the living-memory specification rejects. A reader MUST reject raw API bodies whose index holds such a line, and name the line, the first line being line 1 [JSONL] [RFC9413]. A request or response file that its line does not name, or that is not on disk, is not held, as Claude Code was seen to write a line naming no request file, and a line before its files: what the raw API bodies do not hold is absent (event.2). A reader MUST write the line's event from what is held, and report each file not held as a warning, naming the line.
index.5	The members of a JSON object that share a name, in the index file or in a request or response file, a reader MUST read as the living-memory specification's event.4 reads them: one member, its value an array of their values.
event.1	For each index line a reader MUST write one line by the living-memory specification's event.1, its attributes those of the Attributes sheet and those the Messages API specification writes for the line's request and response. The raw API bodies hold no resource, scope, severity, log record body or trace context; the index's timestamp is written by index.2, the time it stands for not being stated [CLAUDE-CODE].
event.2	Claude Code records its own calls and is natively instrumented [OTEL-GLOSSARY] [CLAUDE-CODE]: it is the instrumentation library to which the requirement levels apply, and a reader of its raw API bodies applies none, by the living-memory specification's event.2.
event.3	These attributes of the event are absent, and a reader MUST NOT write them: the raw API bodies hold only successful responses, with no server address or port and no time to the first chunk [CLAUDE-CODE].
Attributes
Key	Field	Rule
error.type		event.3
gen_ai.conversation.id		index.2
server.port		event.3
gen_ai.response.time_to_first_chunk		event.3
server.address		event.3
anthropic.claude_code.{member}		index.2
